              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+protected+topological+phase"} symmetry protection
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomalous+Hall+effect"} quantum anomalous Hall effect
            – obtained by starting with a simple
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} model
            for a
            = succeed "-like" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphene"} graphene
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-metal"} semi-metal
            and then adding a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+term"} mass term
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interactions"} interactions
            (similar to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin-orbit+coupling"} spin-orbit coupling
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+breaking"} break
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/time-reversal+symmetry"} time-reversal symmetry
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystallographic+point+group"} spatial inversion symmetry
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Consider the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/honeycomb+lattice"} honeycomb crystal lattice
            and consider the following three lattice sites
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi i
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_i \in \mathbb{R}^2
          %div{:style => "margin: -10px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/HoneycombCrystalLattice.jpg", :width => "310px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#DTC"} DTC
          %h3#the_graphenelike_semimetal_model The graphene-like semi-metal model
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} of quantum states
            of the Haldane model is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            of copies of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^2
            (regarded as the defining/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+representation"} fundamental representation
            of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SU%282%29"} SU(2)
          %p
            The un-deformed
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bloch+theory"} Bloch
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            at
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/momentum"} momentum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+vector"} wave vector
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brillouin+torus"} Brillouin torus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi 𝕋
                      %mo ^
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{\mathbb{T}}^2
            is
          #eq:GrapheneLikeHamiltonian.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⋅
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mn 3
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi cos
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mi x
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mi y
                  %mi sin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H_0(k)
                  \\;\coloneqq\;
                  t_1
                  \\cdot
                  \\underoverset{i = 1}{3}{\sum}
                  \\;
                  \\big(
                  cos(k \cdot a_i)
                  \\sigma_x
                  \-
                  \\sigma_y
                  sin(k \cdot a_i)
                  \\big)
                  \\,,
          %p where
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_1 \in \mathbb{R}
                is some real parameter,
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum"} sum
                ranges over the three unit cell sites shown above,
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo ⋅
                      %msub
                        %mi a
                        %mi i
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \cdot a_i
                denotes the canonical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inner+product"} inner product
                (evaluation pairing) of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+vector"} wave vector
                with a position space lattice vector,
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi σ
                        %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi σ
                        %mi y
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi σ
                        %mi z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma_x, \sigma_y, \sigma_z
                are the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pauli+matrices"} Pauli matrices
                acting as
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+operators"} linear operators
                of the on-site copies of the
                = succeed "-" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SU%282%29"} SU(2)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+representation"} representation
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℂ
                          %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^2
          %p
            This undeformed Hamiltonian
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:GrapheneLikeHamiltonian"} (1)
            is a simple but good model for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electronic+band+structure"} electronic band structure
            of a
            = succeed "-like" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphene"} graphene
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-metal"} semi-metal
          %h3#deformation_by_the_haldane_mass_term Deformation by the Haldane mass term
          %p
            Consider now also the following next-to-mearest neighbour site vectors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi b
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b_i \in \mathbb{R}^2
            in the above honeycomb lattice:
          %div{:style => "margin: -10px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/NextNearNeighbSitesInHoneycLattice-220518.jpg", :width => "170px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#DTC"} DTC
          %p
            The full
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bloch+theory"} Bloch
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            of the Haldane model is the deformation of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_0(k)
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:GrapheneLikeHamiltonian"} (1)
            by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+term"} mass term
            of the following form:
          #eq:HaldaneHamiltonian.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo +
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %munder
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mi M
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo +
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mn 2
                          %msub
                            %mi t
                            %mn 2
                          %munderover
                            %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                            %mrow
                              %mi j
                              %mo =
                              %mn 1
                            %mn 3
                          %mi sin
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi k
                          %mo ⋅
                          %msub
                            %mi b
                            %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo )
                        %mo ⋅
                        %msub
                          %mi σ
                          %mi z
                      %mo ⏟
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} mass
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} term
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H(k)
                  \\;\coloneqq\;
                  H_0(k)
                  \\;+\;
                  \\underset{
                  \\mathrm{mass}\;\mathrm{term}
                  }{
                  \\underbrace{
                  \\left(
                  M
                  \\;+\;
                  2 t_2
                  \\underoverset{j = 1}{3}{\sum}
                  sin(k \cdot b_i)
                  \\right)
                  \\cdot
                  \\sigma_z
                  }
                  }
                  \\,,
          %p where
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mn 2
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_2 \in \mathbb{R}
                is a second
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real
                parameter
            %li
              %p
                the sum is now over the above three next-to-nearest neighbour site vectors
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi b
                          %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b_i
          %p
            Notice that (only) for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_2 = 0
            this reduces to the deformation by a
            %em constant
            mass term (which is often understood as the default meaning of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+term"}> mass term
            ”):
          #eq:HaldaneHamiltonianForConstantMassTerm.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (3)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⋅
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mn 3
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mi cos
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mi x
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mi y
                  %mi sin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo +
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %munder
                      %mrow
                        %mi M
                        %msub
                          %mi σ
                          %mi z
                      %mo ⏟
                    %mpadded{:lspace => "-50%width", :width => "0"}
                      %mrow
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} constant
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} mass
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} term
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H(k)_{t_2 = 0}
                  \\;=\;
                  t_1
                  \\cdot
                  \\underoverset{i = 1}{3}{\sum}
                  \\;
                  \\big(
                  cos(k \cdot a_i)
                  \\sigma_x
                  \-
                  \\sigma_y
                  sin(k \cdot a_i)
                  \\big)
                  \\;+\;
                  \\underset{
                  \\mathclap{
                  \\mathrm{constant}\;\mathrm{mass}\;\mathrm{term}
                  }
                  }{
                  \\underbrace{
                  M
                  \\sigma_z
                  }
                  }
                  \\,,
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#PhaseDiagram Phase diagram
          %p First of all, the Haldane model
          %ul
            %li
              at
              %em M = 0
              constitutes a non-trivial
              = succeed "-like" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphene"} graphene
              = succeed "-phase" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+semi-metal"} topological semi-metal
              %em two
              Dirac points.
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -10px 0px -10px 30px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/HaldanePhaseDiagram-220518.jpg", :width => "320px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (graphics from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#SS22"} SS 22
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo ≠
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \neq 0
            and as the parameter
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_2
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:HaldaneHamiltonian"} (2)
            increases, the Haldane model passes, consecutively, through
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                at
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ≤
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi M
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %mn 3
                          %msqrt
                            %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \leq t_2/M \lt \frac{1}{3 \sqrt{3}}
                – in particular where the
                %em mass term is constant
                %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:HaldaneHamiltonianForConstantMassTerm"} (3)
                at
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mn 2
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_2 = 0
                –, a
                %em topologically trivial
                = succeed "-phase" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/insulator"} insulator
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+curvature"} Berry curvature
                concentrated (see
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#BerryCurvature"} below
                = succeed "-like" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphene"} graphene
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semi-metal"} semi-metal
                phase which has been gapped out by the constant
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+term"} mass term
            %li
              %p
                at
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi M
                      %mo =
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %mn 3
                          %msqrt
                            %mn 3
                      %mo ∼
                      %mn 0.19
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_2/M = \frac{1}{3 \sqrt{3}} \sim 0.19
                a non-trivial
                = succeed "-phase" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+semi-metal"} topological semi-metal
                %em single
                band node;
            %li
              %p
                at
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %mn 3
                          %msqrt
                            %mn 3
                      %mo &lt;
                      %msub
                        %mi t
                        %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi M
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{1}{3\sqrt{3}} \lt t_2/M
                a
                %em non-trivial
                = succeed "-phase." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+insulator"} topological insulator
              %p
                This is the non-trivial
                = succeed "-phase" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+insulator"} Chern insulator
          %h3#BerryCurvature Berry curvature
          %p
            A curious property of the Haldane model and its cousins (see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "QAH+system#ChangLiuMacDonald23"} Chang, Liu &amp; MacDonald 2023 §II.A
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+insulators"} Chern insulators
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+curvature"} Berry curvature
            is strongly localized around the (would-be) nodal
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+points"} Dirac points
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+connection"} Berry connection
            is essentially a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+connection"} flat connection
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
            of a small
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/neighbourhood+retract"} neighbourhood retract
            of the (would-be) nodal points.
          %p
            (eg.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Atteia16"} Atteia 16, Sec. 2-3.4, p. 4
            %a{:href => "#DTC"} DTC
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "https://topocondmat.org/w4_haldane/haldane_model.html#gap-closings-are-sources-of-berry-curvature"} here
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/BerryCurvatureInHaldaneModelFromAtteia-220518.jpg", :width => "620px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#Atteia16"} Atteia 16, Fig. 2.7
          %p
            Concretely, the above graphics shows the Berry curvature in the Haldane model for constant mass term, hence in the case that the interaction paramater
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_2
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:HaldaneHamiltonian"} (2)
            vanishes:
          %div{:style => "margin: -10px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/BerryCurvatureInHaldaneModelFromDTC-220518.jpg", :width => "340px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#DTC"} DTC
          %p
            Since blue and red coloring denotes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+curvature"} Berry curvature
            of opposite
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sign"} sign
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+curvature"} Berry curvature
            – and thus the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+Chern+number"} first Chern number
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valence+bundle"} valence bundle
            –
            %em vanishes
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo ≠
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \neq 0
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_2 = 0
            = succeed "-phase" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+insulator"} Chern insulator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_2 = 0
            is topologically trivial.
          %p
            In constract, as
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi M
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mrow
                        %mn 3
                        %msqrt
                          %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_2/M \gt \frac{1}{3 \sqrt{3}}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+curvature"} Berry curvature
            is still concentrated around the would-be Dirac points, but now it has the same sign everywhere:
          %div{:style => "margin: -10px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/PosBerryCurvInHaldaneModelFromDTC-220518.jpg", :width => "340px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#DTC"} DTC
          %p
            This makes it clear that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integration"} integral
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+curvature"} Berry curvature
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brillouin+torus"} Brillouin torus
            – hence the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first+Chern+number"} first Chern number
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valence+bundle"} valence bundle
            – is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+number"} positive
            in this phase. This is the statement that the
            = succeed "-phase" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+insulator"} Chern insulator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi t
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi M
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mn 3
                      %msqrt
                        %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_2/M \gt \frac{1}{3\sqrt{3}}
            is non-trivial.
          %h3#KTheoryClassification K-Theory classification?
          %p
            The established
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+classification+of+topological+phases+of+matter"} K-theory classification of topological phases of matter
            asserts that (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+equivariant+K-theory"}> twisted equivariant
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            of gapped
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valence+bundles"} valence bundles
            over a full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brillouin+torus"} Brillouin torus
            classifies
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crystalline+topological+insulator"} crystalline
            = succeed "-phases." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+insulator"} topological insulator
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetry+protected+topological+phases"} symmetry protected
            phases relavant for the Haldane model, this is the
            = succeed "-classification" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KU-theory"} KU-theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+insulators"} Chern insulators
          %p
            There has not been much systematic discussion of how to generalize this statement to a classification of
            = succeed "-phases" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+semi-metal"} topological semi-metal
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            %a{:href => "#PhaseDiagram"} above
            phase behavior of of the Haldane model under deformation by
            %em constant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+terms"} mass terms
            (i.e. at
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_2 = 0
            %em flat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+K-theory"} differential K-theory
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complement"} complement
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi T
                      %mo ^
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ∖
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{T}^2 \setminus \{k_1, \cdots, k_N\}
            of the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            nodal points
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec k := k_1, k_2, \cdots, k_N
            inside the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brillouin+torus"} Brillouin torus
          %p
            Namely, under this assumption the relevant K-theory charges form the following
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+complex"} double complex
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exact+sequences"} exact sequences
          %div{:style => "margin: -10px 0px -10px 30px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/SemimetalDoubleSESinKTheory-220519.jpg", :width => "750px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (graphics from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#SS22"} SS 22
          %p
            (To see that the boundary of this diagram is as shown, use the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+type"} stable homotopy type
            of the punctured
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus"} torus
            as shown
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "torus#AsAHomotopyType"} there
          %p
            Here the squiggly dashed arrow indicates the mathematical lifting problem which accurately encodes the above statement that topological charges associated to accidental/spurious band nodes (those which actually make for a trivial semi-metal phase, in that there exists a constant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+term"} mass term
            that gaps out the node crossings), give rise to local Berry curvatures whose global topological charge cancels out and thus implies that also the globally gapped phase is topologically trivial:
          %div{:style => "margin: -10px 0px -10px 30px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/LiftingSemimetalDoubleSESinKTheory-220519.jpg", :width => "500px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (graphics from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#SS22"} SS 22
          %p
            This accurately reflects what is seen
            %a{:href => "#PhaseDiagram"} above
            in the phase diagram of the Haldane mode, for constant mass terms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            (which are those that correspond to passage to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} K
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{K}^{-1}
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Karoubi+K-theory"} Karoubi K-theory
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi t
                      %mn 2
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t_2 = 0
          %p
            %br/
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Su-Schrieffer-Heeger+model"} Su-Schrieffer-Heeger model
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomalous+Hall+effect"} quantum anomalous Hall effect
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phase+of+matter"} topological phase of matter
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+classification+of+topological+phases+of+matter"} K-theory classification of topological phases of matter
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kane-Mele+model"} Kane-Mele model
          %h2#references References
          %h3#theory Theory
          %p The original article:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Duncan+Haldane"}> Duncan Haldane
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Model for a Quantum Hall Effect without Landau Levels: Condensed-Matter Realization of the “Parity Anomaly”
              %strong 61
              (1988) 2015-2018 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevLett.61.2015"}> doi:10.1103/PhysRevLett.61.2015
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p see also
          %ul
            %li
              Doru Sticlet,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric+Pi%C3%A9chon"} Frédéric Piéchon
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-No%C3%ABl+Fuchs"} Jean-Noël Fuchs
              = succeed "," do
                %em Geometrical engineering of a two-bands Chern insulator in two dimensions with arbitrary topological index
              %strong 85
              165456 (2012) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.6613"}> arXiv:1201.6613
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.85.165456"} doi:10.1103/PhysRevB.85.165456
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Duncan+Haldane"}> Duncan Haldane
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Online course on topology in condensed matter
                %em Haldane model, Berry curvature, and Chern number
                (2015-) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://topocondmat.org/w4_haldane/haldane_model.html"}> topocondmat.org/w4_haldane/haldane_model.html
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Atteia16
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jonathan+Atteia"}> Jonathan Atteia
                ,
                %em Topology and electronic transport in Dirac systems under irradiation
                (2016) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://tel.archives-ouvertes.fr/tel-02426217"}> tel:02426217
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://tel.archives-ouvertes.fr/tel-02426217/document"} pdf
            %li#Vanderbilt18
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Vanderbilt"}> David Vanderbilt
                , Section 5.1.1 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Berry Phases in Electronic Structure Theory – Electric Polarization, Orbital Magnetization and Topological Insulators
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/9781316662205"} doi:10.1017/9781316662205
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tudor+D.+Stanescu"}> Tudor D. Stanescu
                , Section 7.2.1 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Introduction to Topological Quantum Matter &amp; Quantum Computation
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.routledge.com/Introduction-to-Topological-Quantum-Matter--Quantum-Computation/Stanescu/p/book/9781032126524"} ISBN:9780367574116
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J%C3%A9r%C3%B4me+Cayssol"}> Jérôme Cayssol
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-No%C3%ABl+Fuchs"} Jean-Noël Fuchs
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Topological and geometrical aspects of band theory
                %strong 4
                (2021) 034007 (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.11941"}> arXiv:2012.11941
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1088/2515-7639/abf0b5"} doi:10.1088/2515-7639/abf0b5
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+S.+Sergeev"}> Alexander S. Sergeev
                \: Section 8.3 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Topological insulators and geometry of vector bundles
                %strong 67
                (2023) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.05004"}> arXiv:2011.05004
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.21468/SciPostPhysLectNotes.67"} doi:10.21468/SciPostPhysLectNotes.67
          %p
            Modified Haldene model for
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+insulators"} Chern insulators
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Doru Sticlet,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric+Pi%C3%A9chon"} Frédéric Piéchon
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-No%C3%ABl+Fuchs"} Jean-Noël Fuchs
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Geometrical engineering of a two-bands Chern insulator in two dimensions with arbitrary topological index
                %strong 85
                165456 (2012) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1201.6613"}> arXiv:1201.6613
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevB.85.165456"} doi:10.1103/PhysRevB.85.165456
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marwa+Manna%C3%AF"}> Marwa Mannaï
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-No%C3%ABl+Fuchs"} Jean-Noël Fuchs
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric+Pi%C3%A9chon"} Frédéric Piéchon
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sonia+Haddad"} Sonia Haddad
                %em Stacking-induced Chern insulator
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.02491"}> arXiv:2208.02491
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p Much of the above material follows:
          %ul
            %li#DTC
              Delft Topology Course team,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://topocondmat.org/w4_haldane/haldane_model.html#first-try"} Haldane model, Berry curvature, and Chern number
          %p Some of the above material is taken from:
          %ul
            %li#SS22
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"}> Hisham Sati
              ,
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"} Urs Schreiber
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Anyonic+topological+order+in+TED+K-theory"} Anyonic topological order in TED K-theory
          %h3#experiment Experiment
          %p
            Realization in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
          %ul
